BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Bloomington Libraries
DTSTART:20261020T150000Z
DTEND:20261020T163000Z
SUMMARY:The Cost of Openness: AI\, Extraction\, and Scholarly Governance
DESCRIPTION:Scholarly works are increasingly incorporated into AI systems
 \, often without the consent of the academic communities that created the
 m. Questions of access are therefore becoming inseparable from those of g
 overnance: not simply who can access scholarship\, but who determines how
  it may be used.  One response has been to strengthen copyright protectio
 ns\, limit access\, and constrain downstream reuse. But must scholarly co
 mmunities choose between predatory extraction and defensive enclosure? Or
  can scholarship remain broadly accessible and computationally usable whi
 le research communities exercise agency over how it is used? To explore t
 hese questions\, this panel will bring together perspectives from scholar
 ly communication\, information ethics\, and author advocacy to consider w
 hat an ethical scholarly communication ecosystem might look like in an er
 a of generative AI.PanelistsCasey Fiesler (https://caseyfiesler.com/)\, A
 ssociate Professor and William R. Payden Endowed Professor of Information
  Science at the University of Colorado Boulder  Dave Hansen (https://dhan
 sen.org/)\, Executive Director of Authors Alliance  Dr. Samuel A. Moore (
 https://www.samuelmoore.org/)\, Scholarly Communication Specialist at Cam
 bridge University Library  ModeratorAngie Raymond (https://kelley.iu.edu/
 faculty-research/faculty-directory/profile.html?id=angraymo)\, Chair of t
 he Department of Business Law and Ethics\, Graf Family Professor\, and Pr
 ofessor of Business Law and Ethics at Indiana University Bloomington\; Di
 rector of the Program on Data Management and Information Governance at th
 e Ostrom Workshop
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Scholarly works are increasingly incorp
 orated into AI systems\, often without the consent of the academic commun
 ities that created them. Questions of access are therefore becoming insep
 arable from those of governance: not simply who can access scholarship\, 
 but who determines how it may be used.</p><p>  One response has been to s
 trengthen copyright protections\, limit access\, and constrain downstream
  reuse. But must scholarly communities choose between predatory extractio
 n and defensive enclosure? Or can scholarship remain broadly accessible a
 nd computationally usable while research communities exercise agency over
  how it is used? To explore these questions\, this panel will bring toget
 her perspectives from scholarly communication\, information ethics\, and 
 author advocacy to consider what an ethical scholarly communication ecosy
 stem might look like in an era of generative AI.</p><p><strong>Panelists<
 /strong></p><ul><li><a href="https://caseyfiesler.com/">Casey Fiesler</a>
 \, Associate Professor and William R. Payden Endowed Professor of Informa
 tion Science at the University of Colorado Boulder  </li><li><a href="htt
 ps://dhansen.org/">Dave Hansen</a>\, Executive Director of Authors Allian
 ce  </li><li><a href="https://www.samuelmoore.org/">Dr. Samuel A. Moore</
 a>\, Scholarly Communication Specialist at Cambridge University Library  
 </li></ul><p><strong>Moderator</strong></p><ul><li><a href="https://kelle
 y.iu.edu/faculty-research/faculty-directory/profile.html?id=angraymo">Ang
 ie Raymond</a>\, Chair of the Department of Business Law and Ethics\, Gra
 f Family Professor\, and Professor of Business Law and Ethics at Indiana 
 University Bloomington\; Director of the Program on Data Management and I
 nformation Governance at the Ostrom Workshop  </li></ul>
UID:20261020T150000Z-2426609@events.iu.edu
DTSTAMP:20260922T110333Z
URL:https://events.iu.edu/libraries/event/2426609-the-cost-of-openness-ai
 -extraction-and-scholarly
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
LAST-MODIFIED:20260922T173115Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/height/168/crop
 /1/src_region/0,0,3200,3200/32496_oa_week_2026_livewhale.rev.1789410307.j
 pg
X-LIVEWHALE-TYPE:events
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2426609
X-LIVEWHALE-TIMEZONE:America/New_York
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/heig
 ht/168/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,3200\,3200/32496_oa_week_2026_livewhale.re
 v.1789410307.jpg
X-LIVEWHALE-COST:Free
