BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Bloomington Libraries
DTSTART:20261029T213000Z
DTEND:20261029T223000Z
LOCATION:Wells Library Screening Room 048
GEO:39.171006;-86.5168
SUMMARY:You (probably) Haven't Seen This Before - American Parasites
DESCRIPTION:Dive deep into the archival collections of IU Libraries throu
 gh a screening series of rare and unique archival films that center tradi
 tionally underrepresented narratives and underrepresented motion-picture 
 practices\, pushing on and expanding the spectrum of what is considered "
 cinema."  In an effort to keep audiences open-minded and with limited pre
 -conceptions of these rare works of celluloid\, descriptions of films to 
 be shown will not be shared prior to screenings. Join us based on announc
 ed themes and the expectation that the works you see will fall outside of
  Hollywood tropes\, outside of mainstream commercial expectations\, and o
 utside of conventional filmmaking practices. Trust us\, you (probably) ha
 ven't seen these films before!October Program: American Parasites curated
  by Andrea M. SterlingProgram Description: This program invites the viewe
 r to consider the practice of parasitism\; a primary mechanism influencin
 g structural antagonisms that have undergirded race relations in America 
 from the past through the present. The two short films encourage the audi
 ence to question their current understandings of race relations\, the exp
 loitation of Black labor\, and the ways in which that labor has acted as 
 invisible foundations in the practice of White cultural production.Curato
 r Bio: Andrea M. Sterling is a Bloomington transplant by way of Providenc
 e\, Rhode Island\, originally moving here for graduate school and eventua
 lly planting roots. Facilitation is in her heart\, body\, and soul—whet
 her that's in a traditional classroom\, a poetry night in a dimly lit bar
 \, or contracted workshops across the country as a DEI/Transformative Jus
 tice consultant. She has co-organized the Bloomington Poetry Slam for the
  last 9 years\, facilitates writing circles with Women Writing for a Chan
 ge\, and mixes up tasty elixirs at the Uptown Café.  As a graduate stude
 nt in African-American and African Diaspora Studies\, Andrea's focus cent
 ered around constructions of race relations in America\, the ideological 
 mechanics of White Supremacy and Anti-Blackness\, and the power of a radi
 cal love ethic in building strong communities and liberated futures. She 
 continues to carry these themes into her creative and facilitation work t
 oday.Please register below! Advance registration is strongly encouraged a
 nd deeply appreciated!
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Dive deep into the archival collections
  of IU Libraries through a screening series of rare and unique archival f
 ilms that center traditionally underrepresented narratives and underrepre
 sented motion-picture practices\, pushing on and expanding the spectrum o
 f what is considered "cinema."</p><p>  In an effort to keep audiences ope
 n-minded and with limited pre-conceptions of these rare works of celluloi
 d\, descriptions of films to be shown will not be shared prior to screeni
 ngs. Join us based on announced themes and the expectation that the works
  you see will fall outside of Hollywood tropes\, outside of mainstream co
 mmercial expectations\, and outside of conventional filmmaking practices.
  Trust us\, you (probably) haven't seen these films before!</p><p><strong
 >October Program:</strong> American Parasites curated by Andrea M. Sterli
 ng</p><p><strong>Program Description:</strong> This program invites the v
 iewer to consider the practice of parasitism\; a primary mechanism influe
 ncing structural antagonisms that have undergirded race relations in Amer
 ica from the past through the present. The two short films encourage the 
 audience to question their current understandings of race relations\, the
  exploitation of Black labor\, and the ways in which that labor has acted
  as invisible foundations in the practice of White cultural production.</
 p><p><strong>Curator Bio:</strong> Andrea M. Sterling is a Bloomington tr
 ansplant by way of Providence\, Rhode Island\, originally moving here for
  graduate school and eventually planting roots. Facilitation is in her he
 art\, body\, and soul—whether that's in a traditional classroom\, a poe
 try night in a dimly lit bar\, or contracted workshops across the country
  as a DEI/Transformative Justice consultant. She has co-organized the Blo
 omington Poetry Slam for the last 9 years\, facilitates writing circles w
 ith Women Writing for a Change\, and mixes up tasty elixirs at the Uptown
  Café.</p><p>  As a graduate student in African-American and African Dia
 spora Studies\, Andrea's focus centered around constructions of race rela
 tions in America\, the ideological mechanics of White Supremacy and Anti-
 Blackness\, and the power of a radical love ethic in building strong comm
 unities and liberated futures. She continues to carry these themes into h
 er creative and facilitation work today.</p><p><strong>Please register be
 low! Advance registration is strongly encouraged and deeply appreciated!<
 /strong></p><p><br style="font-weight: 400\;" /><br style="font-weight: 4
 00\;" /></p>
UID:20261029T213000Z-2405054@events.iu.edu
DTSTAMP:20260902T132554Z
